/* ── Approach algorithm (Figure 8.1 structure + narrative from ch.8) ───── */
const PL_ALGORITHM = [
  { h: "STEP 1 — Suspect pleural disease", body: "Symptoms (pleuritic pain, dyspnea disproportionate to effusion size, cough), physical exam (dullness, reduced breath sounds, decreased tactile fremitus; intercostal spaces bulge when pleural pressure is elevated; tracheal shift localizes: away from large effusion, toward pneumothorax-with-tension or collapse) or an incidental radiographic finding. More than 1 million US patients/yr develop a pleural effusion; CHF, malignancy, pneumonia, and TB dominate." },
  { h: "STEP 2 — Confirm fluid & estimate size", body: "Upright PA + lateral: blunting lateral CPA needs ~175–500 mL; posterior meniscus on lateral ~50 mL; > 500 mL obscures hemidiaphragm. Lateral decubitus or ultrasound detect 5–50 mL and confirm free vs loculated fluid (US: anechoic 54%, complex-septated 24%, complex-nonseptated 16%, homogeneously echogenic 7% — the latter three are ALWAYS exudates). Subpulmonic effusion masquerades as elevated hemidiaphragm with laterally-placed apex. If no fluid layering and diagnostic doubt → ultrasound. Assessment of mediastinal position (shift with massive effusion = pressure; midline with opacity = carcinoma or mesothelioma until proven otherwise) is mandatory before tapping." },
  { h: "STEP 3 — Decide whether to tap", body: "Indications for diagnostic thoracentesis: (a) clinically significant effusion > 10 mm thick on decubitus/US, (b) any unilateral effusion without obvious cause, (c) bilateral effusions of unequal size, no cardiomegaly, fever, or failure to respond to CHF therapy. Deferring is acceptable ONLY for classic CHF (bilateral, cardiomegaly, afebrile) that responds to diuresis. ICU patients: prevalence 62%; tap if septic, loculated, hyperechoic, or > minimal — 43% are infectious (parapneumonic/empyema). Ultrasound-guided technique halves pneumothorax (4.9% vs 10.3%) and chest-tube rates (0.7% vs 4.1%)." },
  { h: "STEP 4 — Transudate or exudate? (Light's criteria)", body: "Exudate if ANY of: PF/serum protein > 0.5, PF/serum LDH > 0.6, PF LDH > ⅔ upper normal serum limit. Identify ~98% of exudates; ~25% of CHF transudates misclassified (usually after diuretics) — these 'pseudoexudates' barely meet criteria (protein ratio < 0.65). Rescue: serum−PF protein gradient > 3.1 g/dL, albumin gradient > 1.2 g/dL, or NT-proBNP > 1,500 pg/mL (sens 91%, spec 93%) re-labels transudate → treat CHF/cirrhosis, no further pleural workup. True transudate + atypical story: still consider PE, cirrhosis, nephrosis, urinothorax (check PF creatinine!)." },
  { h: "STEP 5 — Routine exudate panel", body: "Send on EVERY undiagnosed exudate: cell count + differential, glucose, LDH (serial LDH tracks inflammation: rising = be more aggressive, falling = can be patient), pH (blood-gas analyzer only, anaerobic syringe; pH meter falsely reads 0.2–0.3 high), cytology (send 50–100 mL; yields 40–87% overall, near-100% for adenocarcinoma with high tumor burden), and a TB marker (ADA or interferon-γ). Amylase ONLY if pancreatic disease or esophageal rupture suspected; TG if milky; cholesterol crystals if chronic milky; RF/ANA add little (mirror serum); cultures if any sepsis concern." },
  { h: "STEP 6 — Cytology-directed pathway", body: "CYTOLOGY POSITIVE → malignant: stage, LENT score, symptom-driven drainage (therapeutic tap → IPC vs talc pleurodesis if lung expands; trapped lung = IPC). CYTOLOGY NEGATIVE + lymphocyte predominant → check ADA/IFN-γ (TB), CT for nodes/masses, consider thoracoscopy (highest yield: 90%+) if suspicion persists. Negative + NEUTROPHILIC without infiltrate → think PE (CTA), viral, GI disease (abdominal CT), asbestos, early TB. With infiltrate → parapneumonic pathway (Class 1–7). Mesothelioma suspicion (asbestos, chest-wall pain, nodular pleura): biopsy with IHC — cytology alone is often indeterminate." },
  { h: "STEP 7 — The five options when no diagnosis", body: "After a negative initial tap + TB markers: (1) CT angiogram of chest — evaluates PE, infiltrates, masses, nodularity, adenopathy FIRST (Light's strong recommendation); then (2) OBSERVATION if improving, no infiltrate — best for the improving patient; (3) IMAGE-GUIDED NEEDLE BIOPSY of pleura (CT/US-guided Tru-cut — outperforms blind Abrams for malignancy + TB); (4) BRONCHOSCOPY if infiltrate, mass, or hemoptysis (parenchymal lesion > effusion workup); (5) THORACOSCOPY (medical VATS — ~90% diagnostic yield, allows talc poudrage simultaneously) for progressive effusion, suspicion of malignancy/mesothelioma; open thoracotomy is last (25% of Mayo 'undiagnosed' eventually proved malignant). ~60% of truly idiopathic effusions never declare themselves (benign course)." },
  { h: "SPECIAL SITUATIONS", body: "ICU: tap if fever > 38° > 8 h or hyperechoic/septated fluid — 62% infectious in febrile MICU. Draining ventilator-patient effusions improves oxygenation (PaO₂ 82→115, P/F 169→237) and shortens ventilation (3.8 vs 6.5 days). MASSIVE effusion (whole hemithorax): 59% malignant, 23% parapneumonic, 10% TB, hepatic hydrothorax is under-recognized — assess mediastinal shift FIRST (midline = suspect carcinoma or mesothelioma). BILATERAL effusions: CHF dominates but 'Contarini syndrome' (two separate simultaneous causes) exists — consider tapping both sides if chemistry differs or story is mixed; Rabin's rule: bilateral effusions + normal heart size = CHF only 4% — look harder (PE, malignancy, hypoalbuminemia, vasculitis)." },
];

